{"t":1794476966,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"4ef21a1e-7887-4d9b-9f52-b213f1b108ef","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-12T08:51:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 120851\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n351 AM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nSmall Craft Advisories have been issued for all the waters\nbeginning tonight through Friday. Snow accumulations for the\nAllegheny Mountains continue to trend downward\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1)First accumulating snow for parts of the Alleghenies with\n  rain showers and brisk conditions elsewhere, today through\n  Saturday.\n\n- 2) Cooler temperatures and dry condtions expected into \nthe middle of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...First accumulating snow for parts of the Alleghenies with\nrain showers and brisk conditions elsewhere, today through \nSaturday.\n\nShowers and perhaps a thunderstorm are expected today into early\nthis evening, mainly in the 18Z-00Z time frame as low pressure\ntracks across the area. Cdfnt will shift east of the area after\n00Z tonight with brisk NW winds developing and temperatures\nfalling from the low 60s into the mid 50s by early evening and\ninto the mid 40s by daybreak Fri. Across the higher elevs,\ntemperatures will fall much more quickly into the upper 20s or\nlow 30s by Fri morning. Off and on snow showers are anticipated\nacross the mountains late tonight into Sunday morning. Despite\nplenty of cold air to support snow, snow accumulations have\ncontinued to trend downward as a result of unfavorable low-level\ntrajectories. This still looks like a light snow event of 1 to \n2 inches falling over a 24-hr period, which does not meet the \ncriteria for a Winter Weather Advisory. Elsewhere, light\nrainfall accumulations of a tenth to two tenths of an inch are\nexpected, mainly this afternoon. \n\nContinued brisk conditions are expected into Monday with plenty\nof stratocumulus clouds during the daytime hours. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures and dry condtions expected into \nthe middle of next week.\n\nLongwave troughing will remain over the east coast through early \nnext week, although heights will start rising Monday as the \namplified upper level pattern slowly progresses eastward. \nLikewise, low level trajectories will gradually become less \nfavorable for upslope snow showers Sunday. Winds should be much \nless noticeable by Monday and Tuesday as the surface high \nfinally reaches the Mid Atlantic, although temperatures remain \nbelow normal. Given the lighter winds, the subsequent nights \ncould turn out colder in many areas, with widespread near or \nbelow freezing temperatures outside of the urban centers and \ntidal shoreline.\n\nFurther upper level amplification will likely happen toward mid \nweek. While a trough or low could develop toward the southeast \ncoast, it appears that ridging will dominate the Mid Atlantic. This \nshould start a warming trend beginning Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /08Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\n\nScattered to numerous light showers are expected today into\nearly this evening, mainly in the 18Z Thu - 00Z Fri time frame.\nNo flight restrictions are expected, but cigs will be BKN-OVC\naround 4kft. Winds will shift to the NW after 00Z Fri and pick\nup speed with gusts to 20 kt. Winds strengthen further Fri and\nSat with better mixing with gusts to around 30 kt expected.\nBKN-OVC stratocumulus deck is expected Fri-Sun during the day.\n\nNorthwest winds may gust up to 25 kt Sunday, then become lighter \nMonday. Otherwise VFR conditions are expected both days.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds will strengthen this evening after frontal passage with\nfreq gusts to 20 kt expected. Winds strengthen further Fri into\nSat with gusts to 30 kt expected. \n\nSmall Craft Advisory conditions will continue in northwest flow \nthrough probably Monday. Winds will start decreasing Sunday \nnight and Monday as high pressure nears, although marginal \nadvisory conditions may persist along the bay for a time.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\n\nAction Stages are likely tonight before winds shift to \nnorthwest behind a cold front this evening. Annapolis is now\nforecast to reach minor coastal flooding with the high tide this\nevening. A strong frontal passage will bring a prolonged period\nof blowout tides Friday and through early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory from 6 PM this evening to 6 PM EST Friday \n     for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 6 PM this evening to 6 PM EST Friday \n     for ANZ530>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/LFR/EST\nAVIATION...ADS/LFR/EST\nMARINE...ADS/LFR/EST\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794476906,"days":{"2026-11-12":{"hi":63,"lo":43,"pop":56,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-11-13":{"hi":51,"lo":35,"pop":30,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-11-14":{"hi":51,"lo":36,"pop":2,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-15":{"hi":53,"lo":36,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-16":{"hi":53,"lo":34,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-17":{"hi":55,"lo":33,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-18":{"hi":59,"lo":null,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794474000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,8400,7700,7100,5600,5600,null,null,null,null,4100,10000,9000,null,null,25000,null,25000,11000,10000,10000,10000,10000,11000,11000,11000,11000,11000,11000,11000,11000,25000,25000,25000,25000,25000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[9.5,9.5,9.4,9.3,9.4,9.6,9.8,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.8,9.7,9.7,9.7,9.4,9.4,9.4,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.1,9.2,9.4,9.6,9.8,9.9,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[5,5,6,6,6,7,7,6,5,5,6,7,8,9,10,10,10,10,10,11,11,12,13,13,14,14,15,15,15,16,16,16,15,15,15,14,14,13,12,11,11,11,11,10,9